An application deployed on the Kubernetes cluster requires an update with new features developed by the Nautilus application development team. 
The existing setup includes a deployment named nginx-deployment and a service named nginx-service. 
Below are the necessary changes to be implemented without deleting the deployment and service:


1.) Modify the service nodeport from 30008 to 32165

2.) Change the replicas count from 1 to 5

3.) Update the image from nginx:1.19 to nginx:latest

Note: The kubectl utility on the jump-host has been configured to work with the Kubernetes cluster.

SOLUTION
Step 1: Check Current State
First, let's verify the current configuration:

bash
kubectl get deployment nginx-deployment -o wide
kubectl get service nginx-service -o wide
Step 2: Update the Service NodePort
To modify the service nodeport from 30008 to 32165, you can use either kubectl patch or kubectl edit:

Option A: Using kubectl patch (recommended)

bash
kubectl patch service nginx-service -p '{"spec":{"ports":[{"port":80,"nodePort":32165,"targetPort":80,"protocol":"TCP"}]}}'
Option B: Using kubectl edit

bash
kubectl edit service nginx-service
Then change the nodePort value from 30008 to 32165:

yaml
spec:
  ports:
  - nodePort: 32165   # changed from 30008
    port: 80
    protocol: TCP
    targetPort: 80
⚠️ Important: If the service originally had more than one port defined, using patch with the array will replace the entire ports list. In that case, use kubectl edit instead, or include all ports in the patch. Verify the current ports first with kubectl get svc nginx-service -o yaml.

Step 3: Update Replicas Count (1 → 5)
bash
kubectl scale deployment nginx-deployment --replicas=5
Step 4: Update the Image (nginx:1.19 → nginx:latest)
bash
kubectl set image deployment/nginx-deployment nginx=nginx:latest
Replace nginx in nginx=nginx:latest with the actual container name if it differs. Check with:

bash
kubectl get deployment nginx-deployment -o jsonpath='{.spec.template.spec.containers[*].name}'
Step 5: Verify the Rollout
bash
kubectl rollout status deployment/nginx-deployment
Step 6: Confirm All Changes
bash
# Check replicas, image
kubectl get deployment nginx-deployment -o wide

# Check service nodeport
kubectl get service nginx-service -o wide

# Detailed confirmation
kubectl describe deployment nginx-deployment | grep -E "Replicas|Image"
kubectl get svc nginx-service -o jsonpath='{.spec.ports[*].nodePort}'
All-in-One Command Sequence
If you prefer to run everything at once:

bash
# 1. Update NodePort
kubectl patch service nginx-service -p '{"spec":{"ports":[{"port":80,"nodePort":32165,"targetPort":80,"protocol":"TCP"}]}}'

# 2. Scale replicas
kubectl scale deployment nginx-deployment --replicas=5

# 3. Update image
kubectl set image deployment/nginx-deployment nginx=nginx:latest

# 4. Verify
kubectl rollout status deployment/nginx-deployment
kubectl get deploy,svc -o wide
Notes
Zero downtime: Scaling and image updates are performed via a rolling update, so the deployment/service remain intact and pods are gradually replaced.

Image pull: Since nginx:latest is a public image, no credentials are needed. If the cluster has imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent and the tag is latest, Kubernetes defaults to Always for latest, so nodes will pull the newest image.

NodePort range: 32165 falls within the default valid range (30000–32767), so no kube-apiserver flag change is needed.

No deletion required: All three operations (patch, scale, set image) modify resources in place — no kubectl delete is performed.

